      {
       "data": "Out[799]= \\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|}\\hline\\mu  & \\text{\\# states} & \\text{dual Baxter} & \\text{direct Baxter} & \\text{dual QQ, s = -1} & \\text{s = 0} & \\text{s = 1} & \\text{direct parallel at $\\theta$1-4h, -3h, -2h} & \\text{single-Q formulas vs T\\_$\\lambda$/f\\_$\\lambda$ (4 shapes, all c, j)}\\\\\\hline\\left\\{5,4,3\\right\\} & 36 & \\text{0 (229 digits)} & \\text{0 (228 digits)} & \\text{2.32} & \\text{9.82}\\times 10^{\\text{-1}} & \\text{0 (228 digits)} & \\text{0 (Infinity digits)} & \\text{0 (226 digits)}\\\\\\hline\\left\\{4,4,4\\right\\} & 45 & \\text{0 (227 digits)} & \\text{0 (227 digits)} & \\text{2.23} & \\text{8.79}\\times 10^{\\text{-1}} & \\text{0 (226 digits)} & \\text{0 (Infinity digits)} & \\text{0 (225 digits)}\\\\\\hline\\left\\{7,3,2\\right\\} & 10 & \\text{0 (231 digits)} & \\text{0 (231 digits)} & \\text{3.06} & \\text{1.10} & \\text{0 (231 digits)} & \\text{0 (Infinity digits)} & \\text{0 (230 digits)}\\\\\\hline\\text{time (s)} & 109.0357 &  &  &  &  &  &  & \\\\\\hline\\end{array}",
       "mime": "text/plain"
      }
     ],
     "id": "cda452db-9a77-4e2b-8352-716744fa6ae9"
    }
   ],
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 1,
   "value": "### Result of Section 5 (2026-10-02, P1, finite test twist, 250 digits; 91 states)\n- **Dual QQ relation, confirmed** to $\\ge226$ digits for every state. The shifts $s=0,-1$ fail at $O(1)$.\n$$z_b\\,q^a(u)q^b(u+h)-z_a\\,q^b(u)q^a(u+h)=(z_b-z_a)\\,G(u)\\,q_c(u+h),\\qquad G(u)=\\prod_s(u-\\theta_s+h)(u-\\theta_s+2h).$$\n  $G$ vanishes at $\\theta_1-h$ and $\\theta_1-2h$, which is the dual parallelism $v(\\theta_1-2h)\\parallel v(\\theta_1-h)\\parallel v(\\theta_1)$ found in Section 4.\n- **Direct parallelism:** $(Q_1,Q_2,Q_3)$ at $\\theta_1-4h$, $\\theta_1-3h$ and $\\theta_1-2h$ are parallel (identical component ratios), but not at $\\theta_1-5h$.\n- **Single-Q form of the site-1 insertions**, exact to $\\ge225$ digits for every $c$ and $j$:\n$$\\frac{T_{(1,1)}}{f_{(1,1)}}=10h^2\\chi_3\\,r_1,\\quad \\frac{T_{(2,2)}}{f_{(2,2)}}=40h^4\\chi_3^2\\,r_1r_2,\\quad \\frac{T_{(3,2)}}{f_{(3,2)}}=-80h^5\\chi_3^2\\,r_1r_2\\,\\rho_1,\\quad \\frac{T_{(4,2)}}{f_{(4,2)}}=80h^6\\chi_3^2\\,r_1r_2\\,\\rho_0,$$\n  with direct ratios $r_1=\\frac{Q_c(\\theta_1-3h)}{Q_c(\\theta_1-4h)}$, $r_2=\\frac{Q_c(\\theta_1-2h)}{Q_c(\\theta_1-3h)}$ (any $c$) and dual ratios $\\rho_1=\\frac{Q^j(\\theta_1-h)}{Q^j(\\theta_1-2h)}$, $\\rho_0=\\frac{Q^j(\\theta_1)}{Q^j(\\theta_1-2h)}$ (any $j$).\n  - There is no $\\theta_{12}$ left: the localization factors $f_\\lambda$ cancel it exactly.\n- **So every site-1 Q-number** is $\\langle\\Omega|\\Psi\\rangle$ times one monomial in four single-Q ratios:\n$$a_0\\langle\\Omega|\\Psi\\rangle+\\sum c_\\lambda\\frac{T_\\lambda\\langle\\Omega|\\Psi\\rangle}{f_\\lambda}=\\langle\\Omega|\\Psi\\rangle\\Big[a_0+10h^2\\chi_3c_{11}\\,r_1+40h^4\\chi_3^2\\,r_1r_2\\big(c_{22}-2h\\,c_{32}\\rho_1+2h^2c_{42}\\rho_0\\big)\\Big].$$\n- **Open.** Express the fitted clean coefficients in these variables. Check whether the same holds for $[3,1,0]$; it is predicted with $G=\\prod_s(u-\\theta_s+h)$.",
   "languageId": "markdown",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 1,
   "value": "## 6. The clean combinations in Q-language\n**Top-killing is an eigenvalue difference.** The top state $e_1\\otimes e_1$ is an eigenstate with $\\langle\\Omega|e_1e_1\\rangle=1$, so $W_X\\cdot e_1e_1=X^{\\rm top}$. The top-killed Q-number of insertion $X$ is therefore $(X^B-X^{\\rm top})\\langle\\Omega|\\Psi\\rangle$. In the single-Q variables, $X^B/f_X$ is the monomial of Section 5, and $X^{\\rm top}$ is the same monomial built from the top state's Q's.\n\n**Leading-order clean data.** Use the site-1 insertions normalized by $f_\\lambda$, i.e. $X_\\lambda=T_\\lambda/f_\\lambda$. For each multiplicity-1 HW state, take the leading-order contamination and signal ratios $\\tilde k_\\lambda=\\tilde K_\\lambda/\\tilde S_{(4,2)}$ and $\\tilde s_\\lambda=\\tilde S_\\lambda/\\tilde S_{(4,2)}$. They are Richardson-extrapolated from $\\varepsilon=10^{-6}$ and $2\\times10^{-6}$, which removes the $O(\\varepsilon)$ term. A clean combination $\\sum_\\lambda c_\\lambda X_\\lambda$ satisfies $c\\cdot\\tilde k=0$. The question is whether these numbers are recognizable, as they were for $[2,1,0]$ ($-18/35$, $19/105$).",
   "languageId": "markdown",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 2,
   "value": "fvals = Values[fLoc];\n(* leading-order k~, s~ in the f-normalized site-1 basis, normalized by the (4,2) signal *)\nlead1[μ_, copy_] := Module[{a = KSn[μ, copy, 10^-6, φ, ω], b = KSn[μ, copy, 2 10^-6, φ, ω], kk, ss},\n   kk[d_] := (d[\"K\"][[1 ;; 4]]/fvals)/(d[\"S\"][[4]]/fvals[[4]]); ss[d_] := (d[\"S\"][[1 ;; 4]]/fvals)/(d[\"S\"][[4]]/fvals[[4]]);\n   {2 kk[a] - kk[b], 2 ss[a] - ss[b], Max[Abs[kk[a] - kk[b]]]}];\nmult1 = Select[nonTop, Length[hwB[#[[1]]]] == 1 &];\n{t6, lead6} = AbsoluteTiming[Table[With[{r = lead1[μc[[1]], μc[[2]]]}, {μc[[1]], N[Re[r[[1]]], 10], N[Re[r[[2]]], 10], N[r[[3]], 2]}], {μc, mult1}]];\nrat[x_] := With[{q = Rationalize[x, 10^-9]}, If[Denominator[q] < 10^5, q, \"–\"]];\nGrid[Join[{{\"μ\", \"k~ (11, 22, 32, 42)\", \"s~ (11, 22, 32, 42)\", \"‖k(ε)-k(2ε)‖ (size of O(ε) term)\", \"k~ rationalized\", \"s~ rationalized\"}},\n   Table[Join[r, {rat /@ r[[2]], rat /@ r[[3]]}], {r, lead6}], {{\"time (s)\", t6, \"\", \"\", \"\", \"\"}}], Frame -> All, Alignment -> Left]",
   "languageId": "wolfram",
